{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-75202",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The point $P(0,2)$  is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{t}-t\\,,\\frac{1}{t}+t⟩ & =⟨0,\\,2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the components yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-t=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}+t=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second, we get $2t=2,$ hence $t=1.$ This value also satisfies both equations. Therefore,  $P(0,2)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$\n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}-t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}+t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}-1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point  $P(0,2)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0\\,,\\,2⟩+u⟨-2\\,,\\,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
